(4696262, 673591, 68035, '<td valign="top">Great shot Ron, I love the way the runner is captured, both feet of the ground, almost looming over the viewer but with a smile that could lighten the darkest day <br/> <br/>Good strong colouring and a real feel good factor about this shot</td>', 'Great shot Ron, I love the way the runner is captured, both feet of the ground, almost looming over the viewer but with a smile that could lighten the darkest day  Good strong colouring and a real feel good factor about this shot', '2008-05-12 07:11:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(4706578, 673596, 61961, '<td valign="top">Beautiful find.  I love those old-fashioned graphics.  It took me a moment to spot the connection between the auto accident and the space they had landed on (too many interesting things to look at, I guess).  Good job, btw, on DOF, clarity, light, etc.</td>', 'Beautiful find.  I love those old-fashioned graphics.  It took me a moment to spot the connection between the auto accident and the space they had landed on (too many interesting things to look at, I guess).  Good job, btw, on DOF, clarity, light, etc.', '2008-05-16 11:51:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(4711513, 672875, 39427, '<td valign="top">yeah you  have to fix your colors here.  An easy way to get started is to set the ''true black'', and ''true white'' points of the image.<br/> <br/>Try this:  In Photoshop, click on adjustments &gt; levels.  Then you''ll see these little eye-droppers looking things.  One is for black, one for gray, and one for white.  Lets forget about the gray one for now.  Click on the white eye-dropper, and then click on the whitest white part of your image, this tool will make that pixel absolute white, and will adjust all of the other colors accordingly.  So play around with it, here it will likely be the reflection on one of the pool balls.  So with the white eye dropper, click on that reflection.  The colors will shift.  Then do the same with the black eye dropper, use it to click on the black numbering on one of the balls.  Again, the colors will shift, and you will be on your way to getting rid of that unflattering yellow light you have going on in this image.  You''ll be amazed at how much this can transform your image!  Get back to me if I haven''t explained this in sufficient detail or if its confusing, good luck!</td>', 'yeah you  have to fix your colors here.  An easy way to get started is to set the ''true black'', and ''true white'' points of the image. Try this:  In Photoshop, click on adjustments > levels.  Then you''ll see these little eye-droppers looking things.  One is for black, one for gray, and one for white.  Lets forget about the gray one for now.  Click on the white eye-dropper, and then click on the whitest white part of your image, this tool will make that pixel absolute white, and will adjust all of the other colors accordingly.  So play around with it, here it will likely be the reflection on one of the pool balls.  So with the white eye dropper, click on that reflection.  The colors will shift.  Then do the same with the black eye dropper, use it to click on the black numbering on one of the balls.  Again, the colors will shift, and you will be on your way to getting rid of that unflattering yellow light you have going on in this image.  You''ll be amazed at how much this can transform your image!  Get back to me if I haven''t explained this in sufficient detail or if its confusing, good luck!', '2008-05-19 14:45:04.000000', NULL, 0),
